systemd — run systemd as PID 1 (boot mode)

  This addon boots systemd as the container's init. Your shell still opens
  normally; it runs as a session alongside systemd, which supervises any
  services that other addons register.

  The interactive shell is a real logind session (it is entered through
  login(1)), so XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is set, a per-user `systemd --user` manager
  runs, and `loginctl` lists the session. This includes tmux mode — tmux is
  started inside the login session, so its server is part of the session too.
  Only non-interactive runs (csb -- cmd) keep the lightweight path and are not
  logind sessions.

  Other addons can depend on this one by shipping unit files:

    cat > /etc/systemd/system/myservice.service <<UNIT
    [Service]
    ExecStart=/usr/bin/myservice
    [Install]
    WantedBy=multi-user.target
    UNIT
    systemctl enable myservice

  Inspect services from inside the sandbox with:

    systemctl status
    systemctl list-units --failed
    journalctl -u <service>

  Note: booting systemd needs extra container privileges (a writable cgroup
  mount, shared cgroup namespace). On some hosts you may also need to run the
  container --privileged. This is a heavier mode than the default minimal
  sandbox — enable it only when an addon actually requires it.
